# <%= inspect(@prefix) %>

A Phoenix and Ash application generated with tinderbox's SaaS stack: sign-in with a
password or SSO (OpenID Connect), two-factor authentication, organizations with
members and invitations, notifications (in-app and email), Stripe billing, an admin
section, a JSON:API, events over NATS JetStream, feature flags, and health, metrics
and tracing.

## Running it

```bash
mise trust && mise install   # once: allow .mise.toml, install Elixir and Erlang
mise run dev                 # start the services, set up the database, run the app
mise run check               # format, warnings-as-errors compile, tests
mise tasks                   # everything else
```

`mise run dev` starts the backing services with docker compose, then the app:

| What | Where |
|---|---|
| The app | http://localhost:4000 |
| Admin (users whose email is in `ADMIN_EMAILS`) | http://localhost:4000/admin |
| Mailpit, every email the app sends | http://localhost:8025 |
| Dex, the local SSO provider | user `admin@example.com`, password `password` |
| Hydra, the local OAuth2 server behind API connections | tokens http://localhost:4444/oauth2/token, admin http://localhost:4445 |
| Metrics | http://localhost:4000/metrics |

Run bare `mix` commands through `mise exec -- mix …` (for example
`mise exec -- mix test`): a plain shell does not load `.mise.toml` and
`.mise.local.toml`, so it would use the default ports and keys.

## Configuration

The `[env]` table of `.mise.toml` is the local environment. Put your own values,
and anything secret, in `.mise.local.toml`, which is gitignored and overrides it.
When a port is taken on your machine, move it there:

```toml
[env]
PGPORT = "5433"            # Postgres, published by compose and read by config/dev.exs and config/test.exs
SMTP_PORT = "1026"         # Mailpit's SMTP
MAILPIT_UI_PORT = "8026"   # Mailpit's inbox
PORT = "4001"              # the app
SSO_REDIRECT_URI = "http://localhost:4001/auth"
```

Moving the app off 4000 also needs `http://localhost:4001/auth/user/sso/callback`
(your port) in `redirectURIs` in `docker/dex/config.yaml`, then `mise run down` and
`mise run up`; Dex refuses a callback it does not list.

API connections (the **API connections** page) are OAuth2 client credentials that
let a machine call `/api` as an organization. An authorization server holds the
clients and issues the access tokens, and `OAUTH_PROVIDER` picks it: `hydra`
(the default; Ory Hydra in compose, `OAUTH_ISSUER` and `HYDRA_ADMIN_URL` point at
it, plus `HYDRA_ADMIN_TOKEN` for hosted Ory Network) or `auth0` (`AUTH0_DOMAIN`,
`AUTH0_CLIENT_ID`, `AUTH0_CLIENT_SECRET` of a Machine-to-Machine application
allowed `create:clients`, `delete:clients` and `create:client_grants` on the
Management API, and `OAUTH_ISSUER=https://<tenant>.auth0.com/`). `OAUTH_AUDIENCE`
is what tokens must carry in `aud`: clients send it as `audience=` when they ask
for a token (for Auth0 it is the identifier of the API you registered). The tests
use an in-memory `fake` provider whatever `OAUTH_PROVIDER` says.

Production (the release image, `mix phx.gen.release --docker`) refuses to start
without `DATABASE_URL`, `SECRET_KEY_BASE`, `TOKEN_SIGNING_SECRET` and `CLOAK_KEY`
(`openssl rand -base64 32`: the key two-factor secrets are encrypted with; losing it
locks everyone who turned two-factor on out of that factor). Also set
`PHX_HOST`, `PHX_SERVER=true`, and the `NATS_*`, `SMTP_*`, `STRIPE_*`, `SSO_*`,
`MFA_*`, `OAUTH_*` and `ADMIN_EMAILS` values of `.mise.toml` (plus `HYDRA_*` or
`AUTH0_*` for the provider you chose). Run migrations with the release's
`bin/migrate`.

With `SMTP_USERNAME` (and `SMTP_PASSWORD`) set, mail is authenticated and upgraded
with verified STARTTLS. Behind PgBouncer set `DATABASE_POOLER=pgbouncer`. On an
IPv6-only private network (Fly) set `NATS_INET6=true`. `/health` is exempt from
`force_ssl`, so a platform's plain-HTTP health check is not redirected.

## Validation

The application measures whether anyone wants it, in its own database.

`/` is the landing page, drawn from `<%= inspect(@prefix) %>.Concept`: the name, tagline and
description, the value propositions, the pricing, and either a waitlist form
(`cta: :waitlist`) or links to sign in and register (`cta: :register`). Write the
concept in `concept.exs` and run `mise exec -- mix <%= @app %>.gen.concept`; it rewrites
`lib/<%= @app %>/concept.ex` (and generates the resources the manifest lists).

`/admin/validation` holds the funnel to the manifest's `thresholds`:

| Metric | Counted as |
|---|---|
| Visitors | distinct visitors (`<%= inspect(@web) %>.Plugs.Visitor`'s cookie) with a `landing.viewed` event |
| Waitlist | addresses on the waitlist |
| Registered | users |
| Organizations | organizations |
| Activated | distinct organizations with an `activation_event` event (`concept.exs`; the first resource's `<name>.created` by default) |
| Paying | active or trialing subscriptions |
| Active (7 d) | distinct users who signed in in the last seven days |

Each threshold is a minimum count (`nil` for none) to reach within `within_days`
of the first visit. The verdict is *on track* when every threshold is met, *too
early* while one is not and the days have not run out, and *below* after that.
The page also shows the conversion between the steps and the last eight weeks.

`/admin/waitlist` lists the addresses with the `utm_source`, `utm_medium` and
`utm_campaign` of the visit and its referrer; `/admin/waitlist.csv` downloads them.

To record a product event, call
`<%= inspect(@prefix) %>.Analytics.track("thing.done", %{user: user, organization: organization})`,
or put `change {<%= inspect(@prefix) %>.Analytics.Changes.Track, event: "thing.done"}` on the
action (after commit; `require_atomic? false` on an update). Recorded already:
`landing.viewed`, `waitlist.joined`, `user.signed_in`, `organization.created`, and
`<thing>.created` for every generated resource.

<%= if Tinderbox.Gen.Saas.Deploy.target(@opts) == "fly" do %>## Deploying to Fly.io

`fly.toml` is the app, `fly/nats/fly.toml` its NATS server (a second Fly app, on
the private network only). Once, from the project directory (flyctl comes with
`mise install`):

1. Sign in and create both apps:

   ```bash
   flyctl auth login
   flyctl apps create <%= @app_dash %>
   flyctl apps create <%= @app_dash %>-nats
   ```

2. NATS, on a volume:

   ```bash
   flyctl volumes create nats_data --app <%= @app_dash %>-nats --region iad --size 1 --yes
   flyctl deploy --config fly/nats/fly.toml --app <%= @app_dash %>-nats
   ```

3. Postgres (Managed Postgres, PgBouncer in front, which `DATABASE_POOLER=pgbouncer`
   in `fly.toml` is for):

   ```bash
   flyctl mpg create --name <%= @app_dash %>-db --region iad --plan Basic
   flyctl mpg list                                   # the cluster's id
   flyctl mpg attach <cluster-id> --app <%= @app_dash %>   # sets DATABASE_URL
   ```

   In the cluster's dashboard (Connect → Pooler settings) set the pool mode to
   **Transaction**, and copy the *direct* connection string into
   `flyctl secrets set --app <%= @app_dash %> DIRECT_DATABASE_URL=…`: migrations run
   over it (`release_command` in `fly.toml`).

4. Storage (Tigris), named like `S3_BUCKET` in `fly.toml` (bucket names are
   global: if it is taken, pick another and change `S3_BUCKET`):

   ```bash
   flyctl storage create --name <%= @app_dash %>-storage --app <%= @app_dash %> --yes
   ```

   It sets `AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID` and `AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY`, which
   `config/runtime.exs` reads.

5. Secrets:

   ```bash
   flyctl secrets set --app <%= @app_dash %> \
     SECRET_KEY_BASE="$(mise exec -- mix phx.gen.secret)" \
     TOKEN_SIGNING_SECRET="$(mise exec -- mix phx.gen.secret)" \
     CLOAK_KEY="$(openssl rand -base64 32)" \
     SMTP_PASSWORD=<Resend API key> ADMIN_EMAILS=<you@example.com> \
     SSO_BASE_URL=<issuer> SSO_CLIENT_ID=<id> SSO_CLIENT_SECRET=<secret> \
     STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=<sk_live_…> STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=<whsec_…> \
     STRIPE_PRICE_STARTER=<price_…> STRIPE_PRICE_PRO=<price_…>
   ```

   Register `https://<%= @app_dash %>.fly.dev/auth/user/sso/callback` with the SSO
   issuer, point a Stripe webhook at `https://<%= @app_dash %>.fly.dev/webhooks/stripe`,
   and verify `MAIL_FROM`'s domain with Resend (or change `SMTP_*` in `fly.toml`).

Then, and for every release:

```bash
mise run deploy                              # flyctl deploy: build, bin/migrate, roll out
flyctl logs --app <%= @app_dash %>
curl https://<%= @app_dash %>.fly.dev/health
```

API connections need an authorization server in production: `OAUTH_PROVIDER=auth0`
with the `AUTH0_*` secrets, or hosted Ory with `OAUTH_ISSUER`, `HYDRA_ADMIN_URL` and
`HYDRA_ADMIN_TOKEN`. Until then the API connections page says the provider cannot
be reached; everything else works.

<% end %>
## Conventions

* Every organization-scoped resource has an `organization_id` attribute, and its
  write policies use
  `{<%= inspect(@accounts) %>.Checks.ActorManagesOrganization, field: :organization_id}`,
  which reads the id off the changeset. A resource whose organization comes from a
  parent uses
  `change {<%= inspect(@accounts) %>.Changes.InheritOrganization, from: :parent_id, resource: Parent}`
  (a plain change, so the policy sees the id), and
  `validate {<%= inspect(@accounts) %>.Validations.SameOrganization, fields: [other_id: Other]}`
  for any further ids it references.
* A connection token's actor is a `<%= inspect(@connection) %>`, not a user: next to the
  membership check, an organization-scoped read policy adds
  `authorize_if expr(organization_id == ^actor(:organization_id))` (on the
  organization itself, `id == …`) when connections should read the resource.
* New organization pages mount `<%= inspect(@web) %>.LiveOrganization`
  (`:require_organization`, or `:require_manager` for owner/admin-only pages) and
  import `<%= inspect(@web) %>.LiveHelpers` (`find/4`, `report/3`, `manager?/1`,
  `error_message/1`). Pass `wide` to `Layouts.app` for pages made of tables, and
  `current_page` to highlight its entry; add the entry to the `:nav` slot in
  `<%= inspect(@web) %>.Layouts.app/1`.
* A sign-in is finished by `<%= inspect(@web) %>.AuthController`, which asks
  `<%= inspect(@accounts) %>.MFA.sign_in_step/2` whether the user is signed in, has to enter
  a code, or has to set a second factor up (`<%= inspect(@web) %>.MfaController`). Code
  that signs a user in some other way (a token endpoint, another controller) has to
  make the same call, or it skips the second factor. A different kind of factor is a
  `<%= inspect(@accounts) %>.MFA.Provider`, set with
  `config <%= inspect(@app) %>, <%= inspect(@accounts) %>.MFA, provider: …`. `CLOAK_KEY` encrypts the TOTP
  secrets through `<%= inspect(@prefix) %>.Vault`; encrypt other attributes the same way
  with AshCloak.
* Build pages from the UI kit, `<%= inspect(@web) %>.UI`, which every template imports:
  `page_header`, `card`, `stat_grid`, `badge`/`status_badge`, `alert`,
  `empty_state`, `tabs`, `modal`, `dropdown`, `feed`, `description_list` and
  `data_table`. A list page keeps its rows and a `ListState` in assigns and runs
  every `"table"` event through `ListState.update/2` and `ListState.run/2` (see
  the admin users page); a short list passes `data_table` its rows and no state.
  Every component is on show, live, at `/dev/ui`. Style with daisyUI's semantic
  colors (`bg-base-100`, `text-primary`, …), never Tailwind's palette, so the
  themepack (`mix tinderbox.gen.ui --themepack …`) and dark mode keep working.
* Notify an organization's owners and admins with
  `<%= inspect(@accounts) %>.managers/1` as the recipients. From an action,
  `change {<%= inspect(@accounts) %>.Changes.NotifyManagers, key: "thing.created", data: {Module, :function}}`
  does it after commit; add the key to `<%= inspect(@prefix) %>.Notifications.Catalog`.
* `<%= inspect(@prefix) %>.Events.AshNotifier` publishes a record's **public**
  attributes: `public?` also means "goes onto the event bus", so keep large or
  sensitive attributes private.
* The application's own metrics go in `<%= inspect(@prefix) %>.PromEx.Plugins.App`.
* `<%= inspect(@prefix) %>.Storage` has `put/2`, `get/1`, `delete/1` and `presign/2`
  (S3 only; GCS answers `{:error, :not_supported}`). Prefix keys with the
  organization id.
* Grow the domain with the project's own generators (run them through
  `mise exec -- mix …`):

  | Task | Adds |
  |---|---|
  | `mix <%= @app %>.gen.concept [concept.exs]` | applies the manifest: rewrites `<%= inspect(@prefix) %>.Concept`, generates the `resources` that are new, one migration |
  | `mix <%= @app %>.gen.live <%= inspect(@prefix) %>.Things.Thing name:string:required … [--nav Things] [--icon hero-…] [--route things]` | list, show and form pages, routes, a navigation entry and a test (and the resource, when new) |
  | `mix <%= @app %>.gen.resource <%= inspect(@prefix) %>.Things.Thing name:string:required … [--no-admin-bypass] [--table things]` | an organization-scoped resource, its fixture and migration (`--no-codegen` skips it) |
  | `mix <%= @app %>.gen.domain <%= inspect(@prefix) %>.Things [--queues things:5]` | a domain, registered in `ash_domains` (AshOban only sees the triggers of registered domains), with its Oban queues |

  Attributes are `name:type[:required][:unique]`; the types are `string`, `text`,
  `integer`, `decimal`, `boolean`, `date`, `datetime`, `uuid`, `map`, `enum:a,b,c` and
  `belongs_to:Target` (`User`, another resource of the domain, or a full module name).
  Generated resources are written by the organization's owners and admins and read
  by its members; platform admins do anything unless `--no-admin-bypass`. The files
  they are rendered from are in `priv/templates/<%= @app %>.gen/` and are this
  project's to edit.
* In tests, `<%= inspect(@prefix) %>.Fixtures` creates users, organizations and
  memberships, and `<%= inspect(@web) %>.LiveTestHelpers.sign_in/2` signs a user into
  a conn for `live/2` (see `test/<%= @app %>_web/live/members_live_test.exs`).
  `AshOban.schedule_and_run_triggers/2` only runs the jobs with `drain_queues?: true`,
  which raises outside `MIX_ENV=test`; elsewhere call it without, and the running
  queues do the work.
